WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.670
- Perhaps someday in the far future

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.170
we'll be telling our grandkids

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:07.230
about how restaurants
once had human servers.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.450
Instead of ordering
from a kiosk or a phone

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.670
a flesh and blood employee would come

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.730
to your table, offer
recommendations, and take your order.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.132
So old fashioned, right?

00:00:16.132 --> 00:00:17.880
- Yes, it seems like many

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.400
of the food service
technologies popularized

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.160
during the pandemic are sticking around.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.130
Even though the majority
of Americans are vaccinated

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.370
and we've largely gone back
to eating maskless indoors,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.440
we're still scanning QR codes

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.870
and scrolling through digital menus.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.160
Why is that?

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.080
- Of course, COVID is still with us

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.810
and still poses a threat
to vulnerable people

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:42.330
but we now know that you're
far more likely to get it

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.610
from the air than a piece of paper.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.260
The reason we haven't reverted back

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.960
to pre-pandemic dining
trends has less to do

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.710
with epidemiology or even
technology than economics.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.967
Specifically labor economics.

00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:57.717
(upbeat music)

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.180
- It's been called The Great Resignation,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.420
The Great Renegotiation,
or The Great Reshuffle

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.000
and it refers to an unprecedented spike

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.390
in Americans voluntarily
leaving their jobs beginning

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.220
in early 2021.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.370
At first, some politicians
were quick to accuse people

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.250
of being lazy, opting to
live off government handouts

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.930
rather than work for a living.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.870
But as the picture became
clear, economists found

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.613
that most quitters were
not leaving the workforce--

00:01:30.613 --> 00:01:33.630
they were looking for a better job.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.550
- And for a lot of them it worked.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:38.310
In many regions, workforce
participation is back up

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.130
to or even higher than
it was pre-pandemic.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.620
Wages have risen
virtually across the board

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.740
although inflation has
taken a chunk out of that

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.930
and many workers find
that they have more power

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.540
and flexibility at their
jobs than they did before.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.600
- However, there are a few
sectors still struggling

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.193
to fill vacant positions,

00:01:57.573 --> 00:01:59.490
and hospitality is at
the top of that list.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.580
As of the summer of 2022,
food service employment

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.270
is still 6% below pre-pandemic levels.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:10.290
There are two big reasons:
as non-essential businesses,

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.150
many restaurants closed their doors

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.070
at the beginning of the pandemic.

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.540
Since their employees were some

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.700
of the first to be laid off or furloughed

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.141
they were also the first to find new jobs,

00:02:20.141 --> 00:02:23.790
many in burgeoning sectors
like tech and logistics.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.790
When the restaurant industry
finally started to come back

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:30.810
they found that their employees
didn't want to... come back.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.030
- That leads us to the second big reason.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.610
These jobs were never
that good to begin with.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.920
Low pay, unpredictable schedules,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.530
lack of benefits and
advancement potential.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.840
Many of the people who used
to work in the food service

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.970
industry prefer their new jobs more,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.220
quoting higher pay and flexibility

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:49.380
as the most important factors.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.200
- But wait, if these jobs were so lousy,

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:53.970
why did it take a pandemic

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:57.510
for cooks and servers to
finally leave the apron behind?

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.270
- The short answer is that
the labor market is much

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.880
tighter now due to several factors.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.370
Not everyone who quit found another job.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:08.400
Some started their own businesses,
some went back to school.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.380
A lot of baby boomers saw the pandemic

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.850
as a good time to retire,

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:14.460
taking their decades of
work experience with them.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.080
Gen-Zers have less interest

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.090
in food service as a temporary job

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:20.280
and more restrictive
immigration policies have

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.470
meant less migrant labor as well.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.690
The cumulative result is that there aren't

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.300
enough applicants to fill
all the available positions,

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.820
giving workers more leverage and choice.

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.610
- That's great for workers,
but rough for the industry

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.640
and it creates a destructive spiral.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.070
Short staffed restaurants
ask their employees

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.480
to work harder.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.430
Customers are less satisfied,

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.830
giving more complaints and fewer tips.

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:45.960
As the job becomes less worth it

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.050
more people quit and
the problem gets worse.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.150
- Employers are aware of the dilemma

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.560
and many are putting their faith

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.320
in technology as the magic solution.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:59.261
QR codes, online menus, phone
payments, robot servers--

00:03:59.261 --> 00:04:01.890
anything that can cut down the number

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.710
of humans on staff is being implemented.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.870
There are even some
artificial intelligence

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.237
technologies being
developed that can, say,

00:04:09.237 --> 00:04:12.630
understand a customer's spoken
order at a drive-through

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:14.640
or predict what ingredients to order

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.010
from vendors to minimize food waste.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.560
According to one survey, more than 75%

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.220
of eateries now use at
least three automated tools.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.111
And the restaurant
management software market

00:04:25.111 --> 00:04:26.550
is expected to grow

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:30.000
by almost 16% annually
over the next decade.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.010
- But not everyone is on board.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.260
Some food service
employees say that working

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.763
alongside tech makes the
job harder, not easier.

00:04:37.763 --> 00:04:40.740
Not all customers have smartphones

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.310
and some that do aren't
comfortable navigating technology.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:47.190
Servers often have to play
the role of tech support

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.700
and may receive smaller
tips from irritated diners.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.160
Mobile ordering also
encourages customers to

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.160
make more additions and special requests

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.610
while expecting machine-like
speed and accuracy.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:02.430
Servers and baristas who
once prided themselves

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.560
on their interpersonal skills now feel

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.260
like dehumanized vending machines.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.560
- It's not very pleasant
for the customers either.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.590
A study done by social
psychologist Ryan Dwyer

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:14.940
found that cell phone use

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.257
during social interactions
like shared meals

00:05:17.257 --> 00:05:20.940
decreased the enjoyment of
the experience for everyone.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.760
And we all know that using
your phone for one thing,

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:24.930
like ordering a drink

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.180
can easily spiral into checking your email

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.850
replying to texts, and
scrolling through Insta.

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.070
- There are other unforeseen consequences

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:33.330
of restaurant tech.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:35.130
Many of these automated systems

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.280
like the rest of the internet,
collect data about you

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:41.580
and your eating habits and
share it with advertisers.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.620
Some industry experts see a future where

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.690
your menu is tailored specifically

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.750
to you with prices that
fluctuate depending on

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:51.630
what's available or nearing expiration.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.460
While it could be handy to have a menu

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.500
that remembers your food allergies,

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.510
surge pricing for meals has been met

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:00.630
with pretty fierce backlash
where it's been tried.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:02.610
Imagine seeing your steak dinner go

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:05.580
on sale right after
you finished eating it.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.560
- Besides eating out isn't just

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.590
about acquiring a meal as
efficiently as possible.

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.560
If that's your priority,
you're better off ordering in.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.300
As we said in a previous video

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.150
you're paying not just for
food, but an environment

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.030
in which you can have
meaningful human interactions.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:22.740
Some restaurateurs still believe

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.690
that the classic dining experience is what

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.640
customers are looking
for, and the only way to

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.250
save it is to make the
jobs more attractive

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.920
to prospective and current employees.

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.960
- That can mean paying higher wages

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.700
allowing more flexible schedules

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.010
and offering benefits and perks.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:42.150
But at a time when 53% of
Americans have already cut back

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:44.430
on dining out when food costs are up

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:48.935
by 17% and 41% of restaurants
can't even afford the rent

00:06:48.935 --> 00:06:52.710
where will they get the
money to pay employees more?

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.540
Some are raising their prices

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.600
others shrinking their menus
or shortening their hours.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.520
And many say that investing

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:03.480
in an experienced staff is
actually more cost effective.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.220
Turnover is expensive.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.510
You can end up spending more

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.010
on training and recruitment than

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:11.430
on the perks it would take
to retain valuable employees.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.290
- No one knows what will
happen to the industry

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:16.770
but the next couple years are
likely to be transformative.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.600
Perhaps dining in the future will be

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.390
like stepping into an
automated vending machine

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.910
or maybe careers in food
service will finally

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.820
get the respect and
compensation they deserve.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.400
Or a potential recession could erode

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:30.600
labor's bargaining power

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.390
and restaurants will go
back to business as usual.

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:36.750
- Like most economics,
it's a question of values.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.550
How much do diners value

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.920
the traditional restaurant experience?

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.070
How much do employers
value their employees?

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:47.070
- And how much do we
all value face-to-face

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:48.900
human connections?

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.403
And that's our our 2 cents.

